        {
          "id": "the-blessing-of-the-tribes-why-is-simeon-not-named",
          "title": "Moses blesses “the children of Israel” tribe by tribe — why is Simeon not named?",
          "summary": "Deuteronomy 33 is introduced as the blessing with which Moses blessed the children of Israel, and it names the tribes one after another. Simeon is not named in the chapter. The chapter gives no reason, though Simeon is named among the tribes six chapters earlier.",
          "reasoning": "The heading says the blessing is of the children of Israel, and the chapter then speaks of Reuben, Judah, Levi, Benjamin, Joseph with Ephraim and Manasseh, Zebulun, Issachar, Gad, Dan, Naphtali and Asher. One of the tribes numbered in the wilderness and placed on mount Gerizim in chapter 27 has no saying. Whether that is a judgement on the tribe, a statement about its standing at the time, or an accident of the text changes how the chapter is read, and the chapter does not say.",
          "scope": "Q marks a question the passage leaves open. It is not a finding of contradiction or of scientific error. Readings that have been proposed, none of which the chapter states: that Simeon is passed over because of what Jacob says of Simeon and Levi in Genesis 49:5–7, though Levi is blessed here; that Simeon is counted within Judah, in whose land the tribe later has its inheritance (Joshua 19:1); that the tribe had become small, as the two numberings quoted may suggest; that a saying for Simeon has dropped out of the text. Check made: a whole-word search of the 29 verses of Deuteronomy 33 in the packaged KJV finds each of the other names listed above and does not find “Simeon”; the same search of all Deuteronomy finds “Simeon” at 27:12 only; the 29 verses printed on the cited page of Brenton’s Greek for chapter 33 were searched for the name and it was not found. That is a word search of the editions named, not a survey of manuscripts. These checked passages do not explicitly settle why Simeon is not named. The record does not claim that the blessing is defective or that it conflicts with another passage. No H or G is proposed. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry."
        }
